Thank you for choosing Linux CDirt as your MUD code base.  This code has
been written specifically for linux, but should be easily compiled on
BSD POSIX systems, and, with a little work, most others.

configure  --> Run this file to configure and install the game
README     --> The file you're reading currently
distro     --> Run this to make your own source code distribution
cr/        --> C-Dirt support files and complete rewrites
src/       --> Added/modified C source code based on the original dirt source
include/   --> Header files for C source code
cr_inc/    --> Includes for C-Dirt rewrites
src/utils/ --> Utilities which run seperately from the mud daemon
bin/       --> Compiled mud binaries and utilities
data/      --> Text files for the mud, player files, mail, info files...
ascii/     --> Some sample ASCII art
doc/       --> Documentation files
specials/  --> Zone-based additions to the game (for quests mainly)
pending/   --> Zone additions not yet added

Special thanks to: 

Crimefighter, Rhubix, Forsaken and Promised Lands (for debugging)
Mancini (for help with the original Crossroads)

=================================================================

## Docker (local use)

Build the image from this directory:

```sh
docker build -t cdirt:local .
```

Start C-Dirt with a persistent data volume and publish its default port:

```sh
docker run -d --name cdirt --restart unless-stopped \
  -p 6715:6715 -v cdirt-data:/mud/data cdirt:local
```

Connect from the host:

```sh
telnet localhost 6715
```

The image creates the initial `Master` account using the unveil password
`AberMUD`. Choose a different password when building for your own use:

```sh
docker build --build-arg CDIRT_UNVEIL_PASS='your-password' -t cdirt:local .
```

The password must be 1–24 characters. The image runs the daemon as an
unprivileged user and keeps mutable game files in `/mud/data`; the named
volume preserves player accounts and logs when the container is replaced.
The listener binds to the container interfaces so Docker port publishing can
reach it.

`docker stop cdirt` and `docker start cdirt` stop and restart the instance.
Remove the container with `docker rm -f cdirt`; the `cdirt-data` volume remains
until explicitly removed.

## Docker build and run

This build uses Ubuntu 22.04, GCC, Make, and libcrypt-dev. It generates
C-Dirt's configuration and zone-derived headers, then compiles the game and
utilities without running the interactive native `configure` flow.

The C-Dirt flag system divides flags into logical 32-bit lanes. The bit masks
use unsigned shifts so bit 31 remains a single lane bit on 64-bit builds.

